1. NETWORK SETUP

First, the laptop was connected to the home Wi-Fi network. After connecting,
the network configuration was checked to confirm that the laptop had received
a valid IP address.

The laptop was assigned the IP address 192.168.1.69, and the network
gateway/router was 192.168.1.254.

The arp -a command was then used to view devices that the laptop could
identify on the local network. The command displayed IP addresses along
with their corresponding physical (MAC) addresses.

Example output:

Interface: 192.168.1.69 --- 0xb

Internet Address      Physical Address      Type
192.168.1.254         80-ae-3c-4c-5d-xx     dynamic

This confirmed that the laptop could identify the local router on the
Wi-Fi network.


2. TESTING CONNECTIVITY USING PING

The ping command was used to test network connectivity. First, the local
gateway was tested:

ping 192.168.1.254

This test checks whether the laptop can communicate with the Wi-Fi router.
A successful response would normally show replies containing the time
taken and TTL value.

The internet connection was also tested using:

ping www.google.com

During testing, an issue was observed. The following message was received:

Ping request could not find host www.google.com.
Please check the name and try again.

This indicates that the computer could not resolve www.google.com to an
IP address. This is generally related to DNS name resolution, rather than
necessarily meaning that the Wi-Fi connection itself is disconnected.


3. TESTING THE NETWORK PATH USING TRACEROUTE

The Windows tracert command was used to examine the path taken by network
packets:

tracert www.google.com

Traceroute normally displays the different network hops between the laptop
and the destination. Each hop represents a router or other network device
through which the packet passes.

If the hostname cannot be resolved, tracert may also fail to start the
trace for the same DNS-related reason. An IP address can be used instead
to test the route without relying on DNS name resolution.


4. RESULTS AND ISSUES OBSERVED

The laptop was successfully connected to the local Wi-Fi network and had
the IP address 192.168.1.69. The router at 192.168.1.254 was visible in
the ARP table, showing that local network communication was established.

The main issue observed was the inability to resolve www.google.com.

The error was:

Ping request could not find host www.google.com.

This suggests a DNS resolution problem. Possible causes include incorrect
DNS settings, a temporary DNS service problem, or an issue with the
internet connection.


CONCLUSION

The network setup was completed successfully at the local level. The
laptop received a valid private IP address and identified the network
gateway. Connectivity was tested using ping, while tracert was used to
examine the network path.

The main problem encountered was DNS name resolution for www.google.com.
Further testing with the router IP address or a known public IP address
would help determine whether the problem was specifically with DNS or
with the wider internet connection.
